Count the host arm's arena bytes through ArrayBuffer::Allocator::OnExternalBytes.

On the host JS-engine arm no ArrayBuffer's bytes come from the isolate's
allocator: each buffer is one contiguous guest run the device backend's arena
owns, which is what makes it zero-copy for the host. node's allocator is still
where `process.memoryUsage().arrayBuffers` comes from, so the shim reports
those bytes to it -- +n when a run is taken by the C++ surface, -n when it goes
back -- through the shim header's OnExternalBytes(delta) hook, whose base
implementation is empty.

NodeArrayBufferAllocator already keeps that count in total_mem_usage_, and
already has the two methods that move it: RegisterPointer and
UnregisterPointer, which take a pointer they never dereference. The override
below reuses them, so the counter is moved by exactly the path node moves it
by itself. Nothing is allocated and nothing is zero-filled; the previous shape
(an AllocateUninitialized of the buffer's size, freed immediately) cost a real
host allocation of every buffer's size on every buffer creation.

Arm-neutral: on the mozjs arm the allocator supplies real bytes and the hook is
never called.

--- a/src/node_internals.h
+++ b/src/node_internals.h
@@ -132,6 +132,16 @@
   }
   virtual void UnregisterPointer(void* data, size_t size) {
     total_mem_usage_.fetch_sub(size, std::memory_order_relaxed);
+  }
+  // Bytes an ArrayBuffer holds that this allocator did not supply: the
+  // device backend's arena owns them on the host JS-engine arm. They are
+  // part of process.memoryUsage().arrayBuffers all the same, so they move
+  // the same counter, through the same two methods.
+  void OnExternalBytes(int64_t delta) override {
+    if (delta >= 0)
+      RegisterPointer(nullptr, static_cast<size_t>(delta));
+    else
+      UnregisterPointer(nullptr, static_cast<size_t>(-delta));
   }
 
   NodeArrayBufferAllocator* GetImpl() final { return this; }
